1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,100
Agent Office is a local agent handoff prototype
for graph hacks.

2
00:00:05,000 --> 00:00:08,380
It carries the reasons behind a task, so the
next specialist

3
00:00:08,380 --> 00:00:11,260
can resume with current traceable context.

4
00:00:12,531 --> 00:00:14,991
We are working with a fictional release project.

5
00:00:15,811 --> 00:00:17,151
I ask what blocks the release.

6
00:00:17,971 --> 00:00:22,471
The local language model reads task context from
FalkorDB, then

7
00:00:22,471 --> 00:00:23,391
proposes a relay.

8
00:00:24,571 --> 00:00:26,991
These are real tool calls against the running
database.

9
00:00:28,452 --> 00:00:30,392
The release depends on a review.

10
00:00:31,232 --> 00:00:35,172
A policy decision governs that review and the
decision points to

11
00:00:35,172 --> 00:00:35,932
its source document.

12
00:00:36,872 --> 00:00:39,232
The source requires recovery drill evidence.

13
00:00:40,192 --> 00:00:44,132
The graph returns both the relationship path and
the original excerpt

14
00:00:44,132 --> 00:00:46,012
so the answer can be checked.

15
00:00:47,197 --> 00:00:48,797
The relay is still pending.

16
00:00:49,837 --> 00:00:54,537
Its packet records the recipient, selected
dependencies, sources, permissions,

17
00:00:55,177 --> 00:00:57,097
expected result, and return route.

18
00:00:58,037 --> 00:00:59,357
Approval is a separate control.

19
00:01:00,257 --> 00:01:03,717
The model can propose this packet, but approval
is absent from

20
00:01:03,717 --> 00:01:04,397
its tool list.

21
00:01:05,566 --> 00:01:09,886
After approval, the review specialist starts a
fresh conversation with

22
00:01:09,886 --> 00:01:11,066
only the scoped packet.

23
00:01:11,986 --> 00:01:15,546
It cites the same policy and proposes what to
verify next.

24
00:01:16,566 --> 00:01:18,306
One local model serves both roles.

25
00:01:19,246 --> 00:01:22,406
This is a verification plan, not a claim that
the release

26
00:01:22,406 --> 00:01:23,666
work has been executed.

27
00:01:24,891 --> 00:01:28,771
Now, I amend the policy to record that the
recovery evidence

28
00:01:28,771 --> 00:01:33,631
has been accepted, saving creates a new source
version and advances

29
00:01:33,631 --> 00:01:34,591
the graph revision.

30
00:01:35,551 --> 00:01:40,091
The previous answer and relay become outdated,
reusing that old packet

31
00:01:40,091 --> 00:01:40,731
is blocked.

32
00:01:41,987 --> 00:01:45,827
I ask again, the new context cites the amended
source.

33
00:01:46,727 --> 00:01:50,287
The policy gate is cleared while the review task
remains open.

34
00:01:51,007 --> 00:01:54,027
Clearing a policy gate does not complete a task
or approve

35
00:01:54,027 --> 00:01:54,627
a release.

36
00:01:54,627 --> 00:01:58,287
The next handoff is built from this updated
context.

37
00:01:59,497 --> 00:02:05,077
FalkorDB also stores relay snapshots, approvals,
source history, and

38
00:02:05,077 --> 00:02:05,677
agent runs.

39
00:02:06,817 --> 00:02:10,697
Restart testing confirms an approved packet,
survives a database restart,

40
00:02:11,397 --> 00:02:12,137
and a fresh process.

41
00:02:13,197 --> 00:02:17,037
This local prototype uses fictional data,
produces reviewable drafts,

42
00:02:17,617 --> 00:02:19,657
and makes no autonomous release actions.
